  "body": "See https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1196668 and https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1616537 for more fun in this saga of image-sizing interop issues.\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-img-element says:\r\n\r\n> If the src attribute is set and the alt attribute is set to the empty string\r\n> The image is either decorative or supplemental to the rest of the content, redundant with some other information in the document.\r\n> If the image is available and the user agent is configured to display that image, then the element represents the element's image data.\r\n> Otherwise, the element represents nothing, and may be omitted completely from the rendering. User agents may provide the user with a notification that an image is present but has been omitted from the rendering.\r\n\r\n\"represents nothing\" means that per https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#images-3:\r\n\r\n> If the element is an img element that represents nothing and the user agent does not expect this to change\r\n> The user agent is expected to treat the element as an empty inline element. (In the absence of further styles, this will cause the element to essentially not be rendered.)\r\n\r\nThat is not what browsers do as per the following test-case:\r\n\r\n```\r\ndata:text/html,<!doctype html><img alt=\"\" style=\"width: 100px; height: 100px\">\r\n```\r\n\r\nInstead, the spec should say that in that case the image gets a replaced box with zero intrinsic size. I think that would match all browsers after https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=207740 and https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1616537 land.\r\n\r\ncc @cdumez @bzbarsky @foolip @chrishtr @annevk ",
